      "type": "text",
      "text": "{\"unit_id\": \"Sitre\", \"choices\": [], \"reasoning\": \"C1 Ꜣs.t-wr.t is 'Isis (Aset) the Great', made of the goddess name Ꜣs.t followed by the adjective wr.t 'great'. Its consonants can be stretched to fit Sitre: leave out ꜣ and w, take the first t as written, and drop the final feminine t. But Sitre, read as a modern spelling, renders Sꜣ.t-Rꜥ, 'Daughter of Re': sꜣ.t 'daughter' followed by the god Re (Rꜥ). Those are different words that only happen to share consonants, so C1 is a different name.\"}"
